IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clatas-dates`
IMPORT `clatas-common`

-- Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) -- the damages a claimant meets: Part 2 (intoxication), Part 3 (recovery by
-- criminals), Part 5 (structured settlements), Part 5A (provisional damages for dust-related diseases) and Part 7
-- (assessment of damages for personal injury or death, ss 25 to 28E). Asked from the seat of the person bringing the
-- claim: the injured person, or for s 26 a dependant claiming under the Fatal Accidents Act 1934.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts. What the court must be "satisfied" of, or "determines", is asked as a fact ("if the
-- court is satisfied that ..."), never decided here. Every "may" of the court is answered "can", never "will".
-- Negatives (ruled 2026-11-04/05): JUST FALSE where the text says no in its own words ("not to award", "may not recover
-- ... unless", "is not to include", "is not liable", "abolished"); NOTHING where the Act is silent, does not apply, or
-- the case is outside its scope.
--
-- The figures are the Act's own: 25% (s 5(2)), 6 months' imprisonment (s 6(3)), 3 times adult average weekly earnings
-- (s 26), Amount A of $4 000 indexed to the Hobart CPI and rounded to $500, Amount B = 5 x Amount A, the 1.25 multiplier
-- (s 27), 5% (s 28A), 6 hours a week and 6 consecutive months, one-fortieth of adult average weekly earnings (ss 28B,
-- 28BA). Figures set outside the Act are inputs: adult average weekly earnings (ABS catalogue 6302.0, s 3), the Hobart
-- CPI (s 27(8)), the superannuation guarantee percentage (s 25(2)), any prescribed discount rate (s 28A(b): no
-- regulation is deposited) and the maximum penalty of the claimant's offence (s 6(3)).
--
-- Copyright (c) State of Tasmania. Quoted from an unofficial plain-text copy under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

-- Parts 2, 3, 4, 5 and 5A take no accrual date from s 4 (s 4(1), (2), (2A)): where their questions pass a date to
-- `the provision applies to my claim`, it is a placeholder (1 January 2000) that no rule reads.

§ `Part 2 -- Intoxication (s 5)`

-- s 5(1): "If it is established that the person whose death, injury or damage is the subject of proceedings for the
-- recovery of damages was, at the time of the act or omission that caused the death, injury or damage, intoxicated to
-- the extent that the person's capacity to exercise due care and skill was impaired, it is to be presumed that the
-- person was contributorily negligent unless the court is satisfied that the person's intoxication did not contribute in
-- any way to the cause of the death, injury or damage." (2): damages "are to be reduced on account of contributory
-- negligence by 25% or a greater or lesser percentage determined by the court to be appropriate". (3): the onus of a
-- percentage below 25% is on the person. (4): "This section does not apply in a case where the court is satisfied that
-- the intoxication was not self-induced." (5): "intoxicated" means under the influence of alcohol or a drug. (6): a
-- person who took a drug for a medicinal purpose is not intoxicated "if the person satisfies the court that he or she
-- was not aware of the effect of the drug taken".

GIVEN `I was under the influence of alcohol or a drug` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `I took the drug for a medicinal purpose and satisfied the court I was not aware of its effect` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I was intoxicated (s 5(5), (6))` `I was under the influence of alcohol or a drug` `I took the drug for a medicinal purpose and satisfied the court I was not aware of its effect` IF
        `I was under the influence of alcohol or a drug`
    AND (NOT `I took the drug for a medicinal purpose and satisfied the court I was not aware of its effect`)

-- The percentage of the reduction, as a number of percent: 25 unless the court determines another.
@export By how much will my damages be reduced because I was intoxicated (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 5)? If you were intoxicated, impairing your capacity to take care, you are presumed contributorily negligent and your damages are cut by 25%, or another percentage the court decides, unless the court is satisfied your intoxication did not contribute or was not self-induced. Answers the percentage, or nothing where s 5 does not apply.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `it accrued before the Act commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did your cause of action accrue before the Civil Liability Act 2002 commenced (a day proclaimed under s 2)?
      `I was under the influence of alcohol or a drug` IS A BOOLEAN @desc When the act or omission that caused your injury happened, were you under the influence of alcohol or a drug (medicinal or not, lawful or not)?
      `I took the drug for a medicinal purpose and satisfied the court I was not aware of its effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was a drug you took for a medicinal purpose: have you satisfied the court that you were not aware of its effect?
      `my capacity to take care was impaired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it established that your intoxication impaired your capacity to exercise due care and skill?
      `the court is satisfied the intoxication was not self-induced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that your intoxication was not self-induced (for example, your drink was spiked)?
      `the court is satisfied my intoxication did not contribute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that your intoxication did not contribute in any way to the cause of your injury?
      `the court determined another percentage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court determined a percentage other than 25% to be appropriate? (To get less than 25% you must satisfy the court, s 5(3).)
      `that percentage` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, the percentage the court determined, as a number (for example 15 for 15%). Otherwise any number; it is ignored.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`By how much will my damages be reduced for intoxication` k `it accrued before the Act commenced` `I was under the influence of alcohol or a drug` `I took the drug for a medicinal purpose and satisfied the court I was not aware of its effect` `my capacity to take care was impaired` `the court is satisfied the intoxication was not self-induced` `the court is satisfied my intoxication did not contribute` `the court determined another percentage` `that percentage` MEANS
    IF      (NOT (`the provision applies to my claim` `Part 2 (intoxication)` k (`the calendar date` 1 1 2000) `it accrued before the Act commenced`))
         OR `the court is satisfied the intoxication was not self-induced`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF     `I was intoxicated (s 5(5), (6))` `I was under the influence of alcohol or a drug` `I took the drug for a medicinal purpose and satisfied the court I was not aware of its effect`
            AND `my capacity to take care was impaired`
            AND (NOT `the court is satisfied my intoxication did not contribute`)
         THEN IF `the court determined another percentage`
              THEN JUST `that percentage`
              ELSE JUST 25
         ELSE NOTHING

@export How much are my damages after the reduction presumed for my intoxication (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 5(2))? The damages you would get without contributory negligence, less 25% or the percentage the court determined. Answers nothing where the s 5 presumption does not arise.
GIVEN `my damages without contributory negligence` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages, in dollars, you would be entitled to if you had not been contributorily negligent.
      `the reduction` IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The percentage reduction from the s 5 question (for example 25), or nothing where the presumption does not arise.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much are my damages after the intoxication reduction` `my damages without contributory negligence` `the reduction` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the reduction`
    WHEN JUST p THEN JUST (`my damages without contributory negligence` MINUS (`my damages without contributory negligence` TIMES p DIVIDED BY 100))
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§ `Part 3 -- Recovery by criminals (s 6)`

-- s 6(1): "A court is not to award damages if the court is satisfied that -- (a) the person whose death, injury or
-- damage is the subject of the proceedings was, at the time of the incident that resulted in the death, injury or
-- damage, engaged in conduct that (on the balance of probabilities) constitutes a serious offence; and (b) the conduct
-- contributed materially to the risk of the death, injury or damage that was suffered." (2): whether or not anyone is
-- or could be prosecuted. (3): "serious offence means an offence punishable by imprisonment for a term greater than 6
-- months."

GIVEN `the most imprisonment for the offence, in months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is a serious offence (s 6(3))` `the most imprisonment for the offence, in months` IF
    `the most imprisonment for the offence, in months` GREATER THAN 6

@export Am I barred from damages because I was committing a serious offence when I was hurt (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 6)? No damages are awarded if the court is satisfied that your conduct at the time was an offence punishable by more than 6 months' imprisonment and contributed materially to the risk of the harm, whether or not you are prosecuted. Answers false where you are barred, nothing where s 6 does not bar you.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `it accrued before the Act commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did your cause of action accrue before the Civil Liability Act 2002 commenced (a day proclaimed under s 2)?
      `the court is satisfied my conduct was an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied (on the balance of probabilities) that your conduct at the time of the incident constituted an offence?
      `the most imprisonment for the offence, in months` IS A NUMBER @desc The longest term of imprisonment the offence is punishable by, in months (0 if it carries no imprisonment; 1200 for life).
      `the court is satisfied my conduct contributed materially` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the court satisfied that your conduct contributed materially to the risk of the injury, death or damage suffered?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be awarded damages despite my offence` k `it accrued before the Act commenced` `the court is satisfied my conduct was an offence` `the most imprisonment for the offence, in months` `the court is satisfied my conduct contributed materially` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 3 (recovery by criminals)` k (`the calendar date` 1 1 2000) `it accrued before the Act commenced`
            AND `the court is satisfied my conduct was an offence`
            AND `it is a serious offence (s 6(3))` `the most imprisonment for the offence, in months`
            AND `the court is satisfied my conduct contributed materially`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 5 -- Structured settlements (s 8)`

-- s 8(1): "A court may, on the application of the parties to a claim for damages for personal injury or death of a
-- person, make an order approving of, or in the terms of, a structured settlement even though the payment of damages is
-- not in the form of a lump sum award of damages." (2): "structured settlement is an agreement that provides for the
-- payment of all or part of an award of damages in the form of periodic payments funded by an annuity or other agreed
-- means." Part 5 reaches a cause of action accrued at any time (s 4(2)).

@export Can the court approve my structured settlement (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 8)? On the application of the parties to a personal injury or death claim, the court may approve, or make an order in the terms of, an agreement to pay all or part of the damages as periodic payments funded by an annuity or other agreed means. Answers true where the court can, nothing where s 8 does not reach the case.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the parties applied together` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the parties to the claim (you and the defendant) applied to the court for the order?
      `the agreement pays periodically from an annuity or other agreed means` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your agreement provide for all or part of the damages to be paid in periodic payments funded by an annuity or other agreed means?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the court approve my structured settlement` k `the parties applied together` `the agreement pays periodically from an annuity or other agreed means` MEANS
    IF          `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 5 (structured settlements)` k (`the calendar date` 1 1 2000) FALSE
            AND `the parties applied together`
            AND `the agreement pays periodically from an annuity or other agreed means`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 5A -- Provisional damages for dust-related diseases (s 8B)`

-- s 8B(2): "dust-related disease means -- (a) any of the following diseases: (i) aluminosis; (ii) asbestosis; (iii)
-- asbestos-induced carcinoma; (iv) asbestos-related pleural diseases; (v) bagassosis; (vi) berylliosis; (vii)
-- byssinosis; (viii) coal dust pneumoconiosis; (ix) farmers' lung; (x) hard metal pneumoconiosis; (xi) mesothelioma;
-- (xii) silicosis; (xiii) silico-tuberculosis; (xiv) talcosis; or (b) any other pathological condition of the lung,
-- pleura or peritoneum that is attributable to dust."
DECLARE `My condition` IS ONE OF
    aluminosis
    asbestosis
    `asbestos-induced carcinoma`
    `asbestos-related pleural disease`
    bagassosis
    berylliosis
    byssinosis
    `coal dust pneumoconiosis`
    `farmers' lung`
    `hard metal pneumoconiosis`
    mesothelioma
    silicosis
    `silico-tuberculosis`
    talcosis
    `another condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum attributable to dust`
    `another condition`

@export Is my condition a dust-related disease (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 8B(2))? The fourteen listed diseases, including asbestosis, mesothelioma and silicosis, and any other pathological condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum that is attributable to dust.
GIVEN c IS A `My condition` @desc Your condition: one of the listed diseases, another condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum attributable to dust, or another condition.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my condition a dust-related disease` c MEANS
    CONSIDER c
    WHEN `another condition` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 8B(1): "If it is proved or admitted in an action for damages in respect of a dust-related disease (the first
-- disease) of a person that the person may, at some definite or indefinite time in the future, develop another
-- dust-related disease wholly or partly as a result of the act or omission giving rise to the cause of action, the court
-- may -- (a) award, in the first instance, damages (provisional damages) for the first disease, assessed on the
-- assumption that the person will not develop another dust-related disease; and (b) award damages at a future date if
-- the person does develop another dust-related disease." s 4(2A): Part 5A applies to a cause of action accrued, and an
-- action commenced, before or after s 7 of the Civil Liability Amendment Act 2013 commenced (1 March 2014, history
-- note). s 4(2B): "However, Part 5A does not apply to an action commenced before" then if before then (a) the hearing had
-- commenced, (b) damages had been awarded, or (c) a settlement had been reached, "other than a settlement that only
-- relates to damages that, if they had been awarded by a court, would be provisional damages".
-- s 8B(1) is "If ... the court may", with no "only if" and no words of refusal: where the condition is not met (the
-- first disease is not dust-related, or another dust-related disease is not proved or admitted) the Act is silent, so
-- the answer is NOTHING, as for s 8.
@export Can the court award me provisional damages for my dust-related disease, with further damages later if I develop another (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 8B, s 4(2B))? Only if it is proved or admitted that you may develop another dust-related disease from the same act or omission. Answers true where the court can, nothing otherwise: s 8B gives the power only where its condition is met and says nothing of other cases.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      c IS A `My condition` @desc The disease your action is for (the first disease).
      `it is proved or admitted I may develop another dust-related disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it proved or admitted in your action that you may, at some time in the future, develop another dust-related disease wholly or partly from the same act or omission?
      `my action was commenced before 1 March 2014` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was your action commenced before 1 March 2014, when Part 5A began?
      `before 1 March 2014 the hearing began, damages were awarded or a settlement was reached` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: before 1 March 2014, had the hearing begun, had damages been awarded, or had a settlement been reached (other than one only about what would be provisional damages)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the court award me provisional damages` k c `it is proved or admitted I may develop another dust-related disease` `my action was commenced before 1 March 2014` `before 1 March 2014 the hearing began, damages were awarded or a settlement was reached` MEANS
    IF      (NOT (`the provision applies to my claim` `Part 5A (provisional damages)` k (`the calendar date` 1 1 2000) FALSE))
         OR (`my action was commenced before 1 March 2014` AND `before 1 March 2014 the hearing began, damages were awarded or a settlement was reached`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF     `Is my condition a dust-related disease` c EQUALS JUST TRUE
            AND `it is proved or admitted I may develop another dust-related disease`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING

§ `Part 7 -- Assessment of damages for personal injury or death (ss 24 to 28E)`

-- s 24: Part 7 applies "in relation to an award of damages for personal injury or death resulting from a breach of
-- duty", except as s 3B excludes. Every Part 7 question assumes such a claim.

GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim`, `the accrual` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Part 7 applies` k `the accrual` IF
    `the provision applies to my claim` `Part 7 (assessment of damages), other than ss 28A to 28D and 28BA` k `the accrual` FALSE

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the smaller of` `a` `b` MEANS IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

-- s 25(1): "The maximum amount of damages that may be awarded for economic loss due to the loss of employer
-- superannuation contributions is the relevant percentage of damages payable for the deprivation or impairment of the
-- earning capacity on which the entitlement to those contributions is based." (2): "relevant percentage means the
-- percentage of earnings that is the minimum percentage required by law to be paid as employer superannuation
-- contributions."
@export What is the most I can be awarded for lost employer superannuation contributions (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 25)? The minimum employer contribution percentage required by law, applied to your damages for lost earning capacity. Answers nothing where Part 7 does not apply.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (usually the day you were injured). ISO date.
      `my damages for lost earning capacity` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages, in dollars, for the loss or impairment of the earning capacity your superannuation entitlement was based on.
      `the minimum employer contribution percentage` IS A NUMBER @desc The minimum percentage of earnings that employers are required by law to pay as superannuation contributions (the superannuation guarantee rate), as a number (for example 12 for 12%).
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most I can be awarded for lost superannuation` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `my damages for lost earning capacity` `the minimum employer contribution percentage` MEANS
    IF `Part 7 applies` k `the day my cause of action accrued`
    THEN JUST (`my damages for lost earning capacity` TIMES `the minimum employer contribution percentage` DIVIDED BY 100)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 26(1): "Where a person is entitled to damages in respect of loss of earning capacity or where a claim for damages is
-- made pursuant to the Fatal Accidents Act 1934, a court must not award those damages on the basis the person was, or
-- may have been capable of, earning income at greater than 3 times the adult average weekly earnings as last published
-- by the Australian Bureau of Statistics before damages are awarded."
@export On what weekly earnings can my damages for lost earning capacity (or a Fatal Accidents Act claim) be based (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 26)? Not more than 3 times adult average weekly earnings as last published by the ABS before the award. Answers the weekly figure the court can use, or nothing where Part 7 does not apply.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (usually the day of the injury or death). ISO date.
      `the weekly earnings claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The weekly income, in dollars, you (or the person who died) were or might have been capable of earning.
      `adult average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Adult average weekly earnings (ABS catalogue 6302.0, full-time adult ordinary time earnings, Australia, original), as last published before damages are awarded, in dollars.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`On what weekly earnings can my damages be based` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `the weekly earnings claimed` `adult average weekly earnings` MEANS
    IF `Part 7 applies` k `the day my cause of action accrued`
    THEN JUST (`the smaller of` `the weekly earnings claimed` (3 TIMES `adult average weekly earnings`))
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 27(4)(a): "Amount A" is "(i) for the financial year ending on 30 June 2004, $4 000; and (ii) for the financial year
-- commencing on 1 July 2004 and for each subsequent financial year, calculated in accordance with the following formula
-- and rounded off in accordance with subsection (5): A = Ao x C / D" (transcribed from the image civil2.gif), where Ao
-- is $4 000, "C is the value of the CPI figure for Hobart for the March quarter immediately preceding the financial year
-- in which the threshold amount is to apply" and "D is the value of the CPI figure for Hobart for the March quarter
-- 2003". (b): "Amount B is five times Amount A." (5): "If the value of Amount A ... is not a multiple of $500, the amount
-- is to be rounded off to the nearest multiple of $500, with an amount that is $250 more than a multiple of $500 being
-- rounded off to the next highest multiple of $500." (7): the Gazette notice is "for public information only".

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rounded to $500 (s 27(5))` x MEANS
    IF x MINUS `the multiple below` AT LEAST 250
    THEN `the multiple below` PLUS 500
    ELSE `the multiple below`
    WHERE
        `the multiple below` MEANS (TRUNC (x DIVIDED BY 500) 0) TIMES 500

-- The financial year is named by the calendar year it begins in (2003 for 1 July 2003 to 30 June 2004).
GIVEN `the year the financial year begins` IS A NUMBER, `CPI C` IS A NUMBER, `CPI D` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`Amount A (s 27(4)(a))` `the year the financial year begins` `CPI C` `CPI D` MEANS
    IF `the year the financial year begins` EQUALS 2003
    THEN JUST 4000
    ELSE IF `the year the financial year begins` GREATER THAN 2003
         THEN JUST (`rounded to $500 (s 27(5))` (4000 TIMES `CPI C` DIVIDED BY `CPI D`))
         ELSE NOTHING

@export What is Amount A, the threshold for damages for non-economic loss, for a financial year (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 27(4), (5))? $4 000 for 2003-04; after that $4 000 times the Hobart CPI for the March quarter before the financial year, divided by the Hobart CPI for the March quarter 2003, rounded to the nearest $500 ($250 over rounds up). Amount B is five times Amount A. Answers nothing for a year before 2003-04.
GIVEN `the year the financial year begins` IS A NUMBER @desc The calendar year the financial year begins in (for example 2026 for 1 July 2026 to 30 June 2027).
      `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter before that financial year` IS A NUMBER @desc The Consumer Price Index, All Groups Index Number, for Hobart, for the March quarter immediately before that financial year.
      `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter 2003` IS A NUMBER @desc The same index for the March quarter 2003, on the same reference base.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is Amount A` `the year the financial year begins` `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter before that financial year` `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter 2003` MEANS
    `Amount A (s 27(4)(a))` `the year the financial year begins` `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter before that financial year` `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter 2003`

-- s 27(1): "If the amount of non-economic loss is assessed to be not more than Amount A, no damages are to be awarded for
-- non-economic loss." (2): "If ... more than Amount A but not more than Amount B, damages awarded for non-economic loss
-- are calculated as follows: Amount awarded = 1.25 x (amount assessed minus Amount A)" (transcribed from the image
-- civil1.gif). (3): "If ... more than Amount B, damages awarded for non-economic loss are an amount equal to the amount
-- assessed." Which financial year's Amount A a claim takes is not said (finding 2): it is an input.
GIVEN `the amount assessed` IS A NUMBER, `Amount A` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the award for non-economic loss (s 27(1)-(3))` `the amount assessed` `Amount A` MEANS
    IF `the amount assessed` AT MOST `Amount A`
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `the amount assessed` AT MOST (5 TIMES `Amount A`)
         THEN 1.25 TIMES (`the amount assessed` MINUS `Amount A`)
         ELSE `the amount assessed`

@export How much will I be awarded for non-economic loss (pain and suffering, loss of amenities and enjoyment of life, shortened life expectancy, bodily or mental harm) (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 27)? Nothing if the court assesses it at no more than Amount A; 1.25 times the excess over Amount A if it is assessed at more than Amount A but not more than Amount B (five times Amount A); the full amount assessed above Amount B. Answers nothing where Part 7 does not apply or Amount A cannot be worked out.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (usually the day you were injured). ISO date.
      `the amount assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount, in dollars, at which the court assesses your non-economic loss.
      `the year the financial year begins` IS A NUMBER @desc The calendar year the financial year whose Amount A applies begins in (for example 2026 for 2026-27).
      `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter before that financial year` IS A NUMBER @desc The Hobart CPI (All Groups Index Number) for the March quarter immediately before that financial year.
      `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter 2003` IS A NUMBER @desc The Hobart CPI for the March quarter 2003, on the same reference base.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much will I be awarded for non-economic loss` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `the amount assessed` `the year the financial year begins` `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter before that financial year` `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter 2003` MEANS
    IF `Part 7 applies` k `the day my cause of action accrued`
    THEN CONSIDER `Amount A (s 27(4)(a))` `the year the financial year begins` `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter before that financial year` `the Hobart CPI for the March quarter 2003`
         WHEN JUST a THEN JUST (`the award for non-economic loss (s 27(1)-(3))` `the amount assessed` a)
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 28A: "If an award of damages is to include any component assessed as a lump sum for future loss, the present value
-- of that future loss is to be qualified by adopting -- (a) a discount rate of 5 per cent; or (b) if another discount
-- rate is prescribed, that other discount rate." No regulation is deposited: whether one prescribes a rate is an input.
-- s 4(3A): not to a cause of action accrued before the Civil Liability Amendment Act 2005 commenced.
@export What discount rate is used to bring my lump sum for future loss to present value (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) s 28A)? 5 per cent, or another rate if one is prescribed by regulation. Answers the rate as a number of percent, or nothing where s 28A does not apply.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (usually the day you were injured). ISO date.
      `my award includes a lump sum for future loss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will your award include a lump sum for future loss (future economic loss, general or medical expenses, or gratuitous services)?
      `another rate is prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a regulation prescribed a discount rate other than 5 per cent?
      `the prescribed rate` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, that rate as a number of percent. Otherwise any number; it is ignored.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What discount rate applies to my future loss` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `my award includes a lump sum for future loss` `another rate is prescribed` `the prescribed rate` MEANS
    IF      `the provision applies to my claim` `ss 28A to 28D (discount rate, gratuitous services, motor accidents, consortium)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
        AND `my award includes a lump sum for future loss`
    THEN IF `another rate is prescribed`
         THEN JUST `the prescribed rate`
         ELSE JUST 5
    ELSE NOTHING

GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `my injury is one Part III of the Motor Accidents Act applies to` k IF
    CONSIDER k
    WHEN `an injury Part III of the Motor Accidents (Liabilities and Compensation) Act 1973 applies to (s 3B(2))` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `hours a week` IS A NUMBER, `consecutive months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `more than 6 hours a week and more than 6 consecutive months` `hours a week` `consecutive months` IF
    `hours a week` GREATER THAN 6 AND `consecutive months` GREATER THAN 6

-- s 28B(1): "Damages for gratuitous services may be awarded in respect of gratuitous services required by the person as
-- a result of injuries to that person caused by the negligence of another person." (2): "A person may not recover
-- damages for gratuitous services unless the services have been provided, or are likely to be provided, to that person
-- for more than 6 hours per week and for more than 6 consecutive months." s 3: "gratuitous services means services of a
-- domestic nature or services relating to nursing or attendance provided to a person for which that person does not pay
-- or is not liable to pay". s 28C: an award for an injury to which Part III of the Motor Accidents (Liabilities and
-- Compensation) Act 1973 applies "is not to include compensation for gratuitous services".
@export Can I recover damages for gratuitous services (unpaid domestic, nursing or attendance help) that I need because of my injury (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 28B, 28C)? Only if the services have been or are likely to be provided for more than 6 hours a week and for more than 6 consecutive months, and never in a motor accident claim under Part III of the Motor Accidents (Liabilities and Compensation) Act 1973. Answers true or false, or nothing where s 28B does not apply.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (usually the day you were injured). ISO date.
      `I need the services because of injuries caused by another's negligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you need the services as a result of injuries caused by the negligence of another person?
      `hours a week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week the services have been, or are likely to be, provided to you.
      `consecutive months` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many consecutive months the services have been, or are likely to be, provided at that level.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I recover damages for gratuitous services` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `I need the services because of injuries caused by another's negligence` `hours a week` `consecutive months` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the provision applies to my claim` `ss 28A to 28D (discount rate, gratuitous services, motor accidents, consortium)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `my injury is one Part III of the Motor Accidents Act applies to` k
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF (NOT `I need the services because of injuries caused by another's negligence`)
              THEN NOTHING
              ELSE IF `more than 6 hours a week and more than 6 consecutive months` `hours a week` `consecutive months`
                   THEN JUST TRUE
                   ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 28B(3) and s 28BA(3): "(a) the hourly rate is not to exceed one-fortieth of adult average weekly earnings; and (b)
-- the weekly rate is not to exceed adult average weekly earnings."
GIVEN `hours a week` IS A NUMBER, `the hourly rate claimed` IS A NUMBER, `adult average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the capped weekly amount (ss 28B(3), 28BA(3))` `hours a week` `the hourly rate claimed` `adult average weekly earnings` MEANS
    `the smaller of` (`hours a week` TIMES (`the smaller of` `the hourly rate claimed` (`adult average weekly earnings` DIVIDED BY 40))) `adult average weekly earnings`

@export What is the most a week I can be awarded for gratuitous services, or for my lost capacity to provide them (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 28B(3), 28BA(3))? The hourly rate cannot exceed one-fortieth of adult average weekly earnings, and the weekly rate cannot exceed adult average weekly earnings. Answers the weekly dollar amount for the hours and rate claimed.
GIVEN `hours a week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week of services the damages are for.
      `the hourly rate claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The hourly rate, in dollars, at which the services are valued.
      `adult average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Adult average weekly earnings (ABS catalogue 6302.0, full-time adult ordinary time earnings, Australia, original), in dollars.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most a week for gratuitous services` `hours a week` `the hourly rate claimed` `adult average weekly earnings` MEANS
    `the capped weekly amount (ss 28B(3), 28BA(3))` `hours a week` `the hourly rate claimed` `adult average weekly earnings`

-- s 28BA(2): "A person (the claimant) may recover ... damages for the loss of capacity to provide gratuitous services to
-- another person only if -- (a) either -- (i) the claimant was, before the loss of capacity occurred, providing the
-- services to the other person for more than 6 hours per week and more than 6 consecutive months and it was necessary
-- for the services to be provided to the other person; or (ii) the other person was, before the injury occurred, an
-- unborn child of the person; and (b) the other person -- (i) is a person who would have been entitled to recover for
-- loss of the claimant's services if the claimant had been killed rather than injured; or (ii) would have been, if he or
-- she had been born before the injury occurred, [so] entitled ...; and (c) there is a reasonable expectation that, but
-- for the loss of capacity, the claimant would have provided those services to the other person, after the injury, for
-- more than 6 hours per week and more than 6 consecutive months; and (d) the other person will need the services for
-- more than 6 hours per week and more than 6 consecutive months and the need is reasonable in all the circumstances."
-- s 28C: no such damages for a Part III motor accidents injury. s 4(3B): not before 1 March 2014 (history note).
@export Can I recover damages for losing the capacity to provide unpaid care to someone else, such as my child or partner (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 28BA, 28C)? Only if you were providing it for more than 6 hours a week for more than 6 consecutive months and it was necessary (or the person was then your unborn child), the person could have claimed for losing your services had you been killed, you would reasonably have kept providing it at that level, and the person will reasonably need it at that level; never in a Part III motor accident claim.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued (usually the day you were injured). ISO date.
      `the person was my unborn child before the injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person you cared for, before your injury, your unborn child?
      `hours a week I was providing` IS A NUMBER @desc Before you lost the capacity, how many hours a week you were providing the services to the person (0 if none).
      `consecutive months I was providing` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many consecutive months you had been providing them at that level.
      `the services were necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it necessary for the services to be provided to the person?
      `the person could claim for my services had I been killed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the person have been entitled to recover for the loss of your services if you had been killed rather than injured (for an unborn child: had they been born before the injury)?
      `I would reasonably have kept providing them at that level` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there a reasonable expectation that, but for your injury, you would have provided the services after the injury for more than 6 hours a week and more than 6 consecutive months?
      `the person will reasonably need them at that level` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the person need the services for more than 6 hours a week and more than 6 consecutive months, and is the need reasonable in all the circumstances?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I recover damages for my lost capacity to care for another` k `the day my cause of action accrued` `the person was my unborn child before the injury` `hours a week I was providing` `consecutive months I was providing` `the services were necessary` `the person could claim for my services had I been killed` `I would reasonably have kept providing them at that level` `the person will reasonably need them at that level` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the provision applies to my claim` `s 28BA (loss of capacity to provide gratuitous services)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `my injury is one Part III of the Motor Accidents Act applies to` k
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF     (    (`more than 6 hours a week and more than 6 consecutive months` `hours a week I was providing` `consecutive months I was providing` AND `the services were necessary`)
                       OR `the person was my unborn child before the injury`)
                 AND `the person could claim for my services had I been killed`
                 AND `I would reasonably have kept providing them at that level`
                 AND `the person will reasonably need them at that level`
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 28D: "A person is not liable for damages in tort on the ground that the negligence, or other act or omission, of the
-- person caused loss or impairment of the consortium of spouses or of persons in a significant relationship". s 28E:
-- "The following actions at common law are abolished: (a) seduction; (b) enticement; (ba) maintenance; (bb) champerty;
-- (c) harbouring." s 4(3A) dates s 28D. s 28E has no date of its own in s 4, but it sits in Part 7, which s 4(3) keeps
-- from causes of action accrued before 4 July 2003 (finding 6, fork F10).
DECLARE `The action` IS ONE OF
    `loss or impairment of the consortium of my spouse or significant partner`
    seduction
    enticement
    maintenance
    champerty
    harbouring
    `another action`

@export Can I sue for loss of my partner's consortium, or bring an action for seduction, enticement, maintenance, champerty or harbouring (Civil Liability Act 2002 (Tas) ss 28D, 28E)? No: there is no liability in tort for loss or impairment of the consortium of spouses or significant partners, and the other actions are abolished. Answers false for those, nothing for any other action.
GIVEN k IS A `The kind of claim` @desc What kind of claim yours is, for s 3B. If more than one fits, the first that fits.
      `the day my cause of action accrued` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action accrued. ISO date.
      a IS A `The action` @desc The action you want to bring.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I bring this action` k `the day my cause of action accrued` a MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN `loss or impairment of the consortium of my spouse or significant partner` THEN
        IF `the provision applies to my claim` `ss 28A to 28D (discount rate, gratuitous services, motor accidents, consortium)` k `the day my cause of action accrued` FALSE
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING
    WHEN `another action` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE
        IF `Part 7 applies` k `the day my cause of action accrued`
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE NOTHING
